ted search result* with the *current clipboard* or something. Hmm.\n\nLet's re-test the Search/Replace dialog on Regex.md (not todo.txt) to see if extra date appears. That will isolate whether it's todo.txt-specific or dialog-specific.\n\nCurrently in Regex.md with in-editor find bar open. Close it, then use toolbar Search → global dialog? Wait the toolbar search button now is \"Close\". The global search was accessed when editor wasn't in search mode: tap Search (5) opened in-editor find bar, not global. Hmm, earlier in todo.txt editor, tapping Search (5) opened \"Search documents\" dialog. Why difference? Because in todo.txt, maybe the search button triggers file search? Hmm, earlier in todo editor, tap search element 5 gave \"Search documents\" dialog with a result item and REPLACE. In Regex.md, tap search gave the in-editor find bar. Odd. Maybe the todo editor's search opens a different dialog (todo search) vs markdown editor's find bar? Actually Markor's \"Search documents\" is accessible via the file browser toolbar search, not editor. Let me recheck: In todo.txt editor, element [5] desc=\"Search\" id=action_search; tapping it produced a dialog \"Search documents\" with select all, results, REPLACE/OK buttons. Hmm, that seems to be the file-browser search? But we were inside the editor...\n\nWait, maybe I mis-tapped: In the todo editor observe, element [5] was \"Search\" at (922,136) and [6] More options. I tapped element 5 → got \"Search documents\". That dialog is Markor's \"search in files\" (find in all files). Hmm, but the title \"Search documents\" suggests global file search. Its REPLACE button then opens \"Search / Replace\" dialog (search_input/replace_input) — indeed a generic search-replace across documents? That seems plausible: Markor has \"Search documents\" (globally) dialog where you can search files, and REPLACE opens a search/replace dialog.\n\nHmm, actually Markor's file browser has a search action that searches files (content). The \"REPLACE\" button in that dialog opens a search-replace dialog that replaces within... current document? Let me think.\n\nIn Markor, there's an \"action_search\" in the editor that opens the in-editor find bar (as we saw in Regex.md). But in todo.txt editor, tapping search opened \"Search documents\". Why? Maybe because todo.txt editor uses a different \"ViewMode\"/fragment and the search button is the global one? Hmm, or maybe I tapped the wrong button? Let me re-examine: In todo editor, after back from keyboard, elements listed [4] \"View Mode\", [5] \"Search\", [6] More options. Yes I tapped [5]. The result was the \"Search documents\" dialog. So in todo.txt editor the search button is the file-content search? Actually maybe todo.txt uses the \"document\" search dialog (legacy dialog) whereas markdown uses the newer find bar? Could be based on file type: Markor has two search implementations, one for todo.txt? Hmm.\n\nAnyway, the changed area \"Search and replace dialog: the input fields are now built from a shared layout.\" This likely refers to one specific dialog with fields search_input and replace_input. That's the one in todo.txt. So the observed extra date insertion is likely the injected bug in that dialog! But how does shared layout cause date insertion? Maybe the replace field's text got \"2026-11-14\" because the shared layout includes an invisible/extra EditText bound to the date? Hmm.\n\nWait wait. Let me reconsider: Maybe the extra \"2026-11-14 \" wasn't from replace at all. Maybe when I tapped \"Replace (1)\", the dialog closed and a *keyboard suggestion/date* got entered into the editor because the keyboard was still focused on the editor and the \"Replace\" button tap actually passed through? No.\n\nLet me just isolate: do the same on todo.txt from a clean state and observe carefully. Actually, let me first undo the Regex change (Ctrl+Z) to restore \"cat@home\". Tap undo button? The undo is disabled per observation [38] disabled. Interesting: after replace, undo is disabled. Hmm, in Regex2... no. Let me use the in-editor replace's own inverse? We can replace \"dog\" back to \"cat\" using the dialog. That restores data. Do that: clear search, type dog, replace field cat, replace. Or simply use undo in editor toolbar if enabled. Let me close find bar first."}
