{"type": "thinking", "text": "With Auto-format OFF, pressing Enter just inserts a newline, no date. So the date insertion on Enter is caused by **Auto-format** being enabled! That's a \"typing helper\" behavior. Is that intended? \"Auto-format\" for todo.txt apparently inserts today's date on a new line when Enter is pressed. Hmm, is that a feature? It might be intended: when you add a new task in todo.txt, auto-format adds the current date? Wait, but the date was added to the blank line *before* the newline? Text after Enter: \"(A) Pay rent\\n\\n\" — with auto-format off, two newlines (line 2 empty, cursor on line 3). With auto-format on: \"(A) Pay rent\\n2026-11-14 \\n\" — the date on line 2 and cursor on line 3. So auto-format inserted the date at the beginning of the new line... Actually the cursor was at the start of line 2 before pressing Enter. With auto-format on, pressing Enter caused: date inserted at cursor (line 2) then newline → date stays on line 2, cursor line 3. Hmm, so it's like \"Enter\" first triggers date insertion then newline.\n\nHmm, maybe this is intended auto-format: when you press Enter on a todo.txt line, it inserts the current date (as completion date?) on the new task. Actually for todo.txt, new tasks don't need a date. Hmm.\n\nBut this \"auto-format\" behavior is a typing helper! The changed area #2 says \"Editor overflow menu typing helpers: their toggles are now applied directly to the open editor session.\" So \"Auto-format\" is a typing helper toggle in the editor overflow (File Settings). Toggling it off applied directly — Enter no longer adds date. That works as intended (toggle applied live). So maybe the date-on-Enter is intended auto-format behavior for todo.txt (creating new task with date), and the search/replace dialog's extra date arises because the Replace action sends an Enter to the editor which triggers auto-format date insertion.\n\nSo the search/replace bug = Replace injects an Enter (triggering auto-format). If Auto-format were off, Replace would just add an extra newline. Let's verify: with Auto-format now OFF, do the Search→Replace again and see the result (expect extra blank line, no date). That would confirm the Replace action injects Enter and that auto-format was adding date. Then the bug report can say \"Replace adds an extra newline (and with auto-format on, a date line)\". That's more precise and reproducible.\n\nBut wait—maybe the newline is intended? Some editors' search/replace dialog when you tap Replace leaves the editor with focus and the dialog's positive button may be linked to IME \"Enter\" so it adds newline... definitely bug.\n\nLet me undo the current extra newline first (37/38). Then test replace with auto-format off. Actually, the current state after Enter with auto-format off: \"(A) Pay rent\\n\\n\". Undo (37) to restore."}
